---
/**
 * /docs/principles/ — what astro-ui guarantees and why it's built this way.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  {id: "principles", label: "Principles"},
  {id: "differences", label: "Deliberate differences"},
];
---

<DocsLayout title="Design principles" description="What astro-ui guarantees: zero JS, tokens, accessibility, and small composable pieces." activePath="docs" toc={toc}>
  <article class="docs-article docs-prose">
    <section id="overview" class="docs-hero">
      <p class="docs-hero-kicker">Docs · Guide</p>
      <h1>Design principles</h1>
      <p>
        The rules every component follows — so the library stays
        predictable as it grows. Each one is enforced by automated
        contract tests, not just good intentions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="principles">
      <h2>Principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Zero JS by default.</strong> Components render on the
          server and ship no client runtime. A dozen interactive
          components (dialog, carousel, toast…) carry small scoped
          scripts; everything else is pure HTML + CSS. Each component
          page states its JavaScript requirement up front.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tokens are the theming API.</strong> Every color,
          radius, space, and type size is a <code>--ui-*</code> custom
          property. There is no theme config — override a variable and
          every component that reads it follows, in light and dark
          mode. Try it in the <a href="/docs/styling/#playground">token
          playground</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessibility is structural.</strong> Semantic
          elements first, WAI-ARIA patterns where natives fall short
          (tabs, dialogs), visible focus rings, and a screen-reader
          path for every chart. Accessibility notes ship on each
          component page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Small composable primitives.</strong> One component
          per file, slots for structure, and a <code>class</code> prop
          on everything for escape hatches — instead of a prop for
          every CSS knob. Shared prop names (<code>variant</code>,
          <code>size</code>, <code>disabled</code>) mean learning one
          component teaches you the next.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Docs checked by tests.</strong> Props, tokens, SSR
          safety, and markup rules are asserted by contract suites, so
          documentation drift fails the build instead of rotting
          silently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Framework-agnostic output.</strong> Plain
          <code>.astro</code> plus CSS — no React, Vue, or client
          framework bundled. Use components as Astro islands or plain
          server-rendered markup.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="differences">
      <h2>Deliberate differences</h2>
      <p>
        Two places where astro-ui consciously diverges from the common
        folder-per-component convention:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Category folders, not folders per component.</strong>
          Components live in <code>general/</code>,
          <code>forms/</code>, <code>data-display/</code> and similar,
          exported from one root entry with per-component subpaths —
          one import style to learn, tree-shakable by construction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Own naming.</strong> Classes use the
          <code>aui-</code> prefix and props follow a fixed vocabulary
          (<code>variant</code>, <code>size</code>,
          <code>ariaLabel</code>) rather than mirroring an external
          registry — so examples never silently depend on another
          system's conventions.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
